  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EGeneral practice provides continuity of care for people with heart failure, particularly after a new diagnosis or hospital admission. The post-discharge period carries a high risk of readmission, so early review is important, ideally within 7 to 14 days.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EFollow-Up Consultation\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EA follow-up consultation should include:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESymptoms, functional capacity and any recent emergency presentations\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECurrent weight compared with the documented target or discharge weight\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBlood pressure, heart rate and rhythm, and signs of congestion\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EElectrolytes and renal function, particularly after medication changes\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EMedication reconciliation against the discharge summary\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPlanned titration steps and who is responsible for them\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EComorbidities and Preventive Care\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EGPs can also manage conditions that commonly affect outcomes, including hypertension, coronary artery disease, atrial fibrillation, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, chronic lung disease, sleep apnoea, anaemia, iron deficiency and depression. Preventive care should include recommended vaccinations, such as influenza, pneumococcal and COVID-19, along with support for smoking cessation and safe alcohol use.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ESelf-Management Support\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESelf-management education should cover daily weights, symptom monitoring, medicine routines and a written action plan. Referral to a cardiac rehabilitation or heart failure service should be considered where available.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhen to Refer\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESpecialist advice or referral is appropriate for a new or uncertain diagnosis, recurrent admissions, persistent symptoms despite treatment, difficulty titrating medicines, significant renal or potassium problems, or possible device therapy. Conversations about goals of care and advance care planning should also occur as the condition progresses.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </bodyContent>
